diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage. The BGP subsystem is healthy and the 200.1.1.0/24 prefix from router2 is present on router1. Evidence:
- End-to-end reachability works: pc1 (195.11.14.2) can ping pc2 (200.1.1.2) with 0% loss, and vice versa.
- Router1’s routing table contains the BGP route to the partner network: “B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, eth0”.
- BGP is established on both routers. Router1 shows its neighbor 193.10.11.2 (AS 2) up, receiving 1 prefix and sending 2. Router2 shows the peer 193.10.11.1 (AS 1) up, also receiving 1 and sending 2.
- Router1’s BGP table includes 200.1.1.0/24 learned from AS 2 (“path 2 i”). Router2’s “advertised-routes” towards 193.10.11.1 explicitly lists 200.1.1.0/24.
These data localize the working path across router1 eth0 to router2 eth0, with FRR bgpd processes active and exchanging routes normally. The user-reported symptom (missing advertisement from router2) is not present now. Root cause: no active fault detected; the issue was likely transient or outside the network (e.g., endpoint/host or timing of earlier reconvergence). Corrective fix: no restart is required on any device. Recommend closing the ticket and optionally monitoring BGP session stability/logs for intermittents rather than restarting routers.
